Two properties generalize across models. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity at comparable or better accuracy at function level; at repo level, aggregation penalty is configuration-dependent (1.36--19\pp{}). Second, classification yields 217$\times$ throughput over generation. Error complementarity at function level (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%) further motivates instance-level routing; whether this complementarity holds at repository level, where \cwm{} accuracy falls below majority baseline, remains open.

\paragraph{Practical recommendations.} At function level, all three formulations achieve equivalent accuracy; \cls{} is preferred for its 217$\times$ throughput advantage and per-test diagnostic signal. At repository level under standard LoRA fine-tuning (${\leq}$8B), \cls{} is strongly preferred: \cwm{} falls below majority baseline due to output space complexity (19--26\pp{} gap). Generation-based verification at repo level requires either larger models or execution-trace pretraining (\S\ref{sec:threats}). When per-test diagnostic granularity is not needed, trajectory-level scoring offers a competitive alternative with no aggregation penalty.

